course: dictionary
lesson: 14313
title: Formal words that show something is wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound serious because they appear in formal writing, such as rules, letters and news reports. «Objectionable» is one of them. Look at its clue: it comes from the idea of object, which means to say you do not agree. So an objectionable thing is one that people would object to.
explain: «The film had objectionable language, so it was not shown to the younger children.» The sentence tells you the language was not suitable for young children, so it must be offensive or unpleasant. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The film had unpleasant, offensive language, so it was not shown to the younger children." It still makes sense.
explain: Objectionable is often used for behaviour, language or content, especially online. Ask yourself: would most people say no to this, or complain about it? If yes, the word is probably objectionable.
fact: «Objectionable» means unpleasant or offensive, so people object to it.
fact: It is a formal word, used in polite or serious writing, such as rules and letters.
fact: Object is the clue: people object when they say they do not agree with something.
fact: «Objectionable behaviour» means behaviour that is rude or unacceptable.
fact: Objectionable can describe online content that is offensive or unsuitable for children.
exercise: «The film had objectionable language, so it was not shown to the younger children.» What does objectionable mean? | *Unpleasant or offensive, so people object to it | Quiet and calm | Very old and dusty | Funny and light-hearted
exercise: «Dan's objectionable behaviour at the party made his host ask him to leave.» What does the sentence suggest about Dan's behaviour? | It was the best part of the party | *It was rude and unacceptable | It was too quiet to notice | It was kind and helpful
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to objectionable? | Ancient | Barely | *Offensive | Delicious
test: «Parents may object to content that is objectionable for young children.» What does objectionable mean here? | Cheerful and bright | *Unpleasant or offensive | Very large
test: Which word would you use for rude jokes that hurt people? | Drooped | Barely | *Objectionable | Luminous
exam: Mia says, «Objectionable means fun.» Which answer is right? | Objectionable means fun, as Mia said | Objectionable means very old | *Objectionable means unpleasant or offensive, so people object to it | Objectionable means giving out light
